<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otrar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Farab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otrar was a medieval oasis city on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, an important town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. In 1218 its governor massacred a caravan of merchants sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, the incident that provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-the-khwarazmian-empire">Mongol invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire</a>; the city fell after a long siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> died there in February 1405 as he marched to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its ruins are now an archaeological site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otrar.jpg" alt="Otrar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Coopypasted · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%93%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D0%9E%D1%82%D1%8B%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%80_%D1%81_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%85%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Otrar, also called Farab, lay at the junction of two great rivers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, at the centre of a large oasis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> supplied water for irrigation, and the oasis lay near the foothills of the Karatau Mountains, which made it a supporting fortress for the nomads of the steppes. Roads ran from Otrar along the Arys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Taraz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, along the Syr Darya up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Shash</a>, Sogd, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, and across the Kyzylkum desert westward to Khorezm, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The oasis is a series of towns, not one site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, when it was known as Parab. In the 9th and 10th centuries it submitted first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, and minted its own coinage. In 1218 a Mongol trade caravan of about 450 men, including an ambassador of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, reached Otrar. Its governor Inalchuq, an uncle of Sultan Muhammad II of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, accused them of spying and, with the Sultan's assent, executed the whole caravan. Genghis Khan besieged Otrar for five months in 1219, breached its walls, executed Inalchuq and massacred the inhabitants.</p><p>Many towns of the oasis were abandoned, but Otrar rose again and by the middle of the 13th century was a large trade centre between West and East. In February 1405 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> caught a cold while gathering troops at Otrar and died in one of its palaces. In the 16th and 17th centuries the Kazakh Khanate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> lords feuded over the area. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> lost its importance, so did the city, and the irrigation system fell out of use. By the end of the 18th century only 40 families remained, against perhaps 5,000 to 7,000 between the 14th and 17th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The oasis lies in the Kusulkum district of South Kazakhstan Province, 120 km northwest of Shymkent and 50 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>. Only ruins overgrown with grass remain. The settlement covers about 2 square kilometres, and its lowest layers date from the 1st century CE. The central fortress and the fortified city formed a five-cornered hill about 18 metres high. Excavations show a densely populated town with monumental buildings, two bathhouses of the 9th to 12th centuries and evidence that Otrar was a centre of ceramic production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otrar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the governor of <b>Otrar</b> massacred a Mongol-sponsored caravan in 1218, the shah executed the envoy sent to demand redress, and a devastating invasion followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>That arrangement bred the succession struggles which began tearing the empire apart after his death at <b>Otrar</b> in February 1405, as he marched to invade Ming China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
